@namespace AuthWithAdmin.Client.Components

@*
    MKindTitle — "<strong>בקשה:</strong> בעיה בהתחברות ל-API של ספק התשלומים"

    ONE ROW TITLE, TWO WEIGHTS. The kind moved into the title when דורש פעולה's
    separate type badge was removed, so a row reads as one sentence rather than
    a pill plus a phrase. This gives that sentence its hierarchy back without
    giving the kind a colour, a chip or a column of its own: the label that says
    WHAT the item is leads in bold, and the item's own name keeps the weight the
    row has always drawn it at.

    NOTHING BUT WEIGHT CHANGES. Same text, same order, same size, same line
    height, same row. The two spans sit in one bidi paragraph — inline elements
    with no `unicode-bidi` of their own — so an RTL title carrying Latin ("…ל-API
    של ספק התשלומים") resolves exactly as it did when this was a single string.

    WHERE THE PREFIX COMES FROM, in order:

      1. TypeLabel, when the host has it as structured data. Both דורש פעולה
         hosts carry it on their WorkItem record, so the common case never
         parses a Hebrew string to find the kind.

      2. The title's OWN leading label, when it has one. Some request titles
         already open with one — "שינוי היקף: תמיכה במכשירי מדידה לבישים",
         "תמיכה טכנית: …" — and those rows deliberately show no TypeLabel at
         all, because prepending would print "בקשה: שינוי היקף: …". That label
         is the row's kind as far as the reader is concerned, so it takes the
         bold. It is found by position, not by matching any word: the first
         colon inside the opening OwnLabelWindow characters.

    THE SPLIT RULE IS THE HOSTS' OWN, MOVED HERE RATHER THAN REWRITTEN.
    TitleWithKind/HasOwnLabel/OwnLabelWindow were duplicated verbatim in
    OverviewDashboardPage and MentorHomePage; both now render this instead, so
    the Lecturer, Admin and Mentor cards cannot drift apart. The behaviour is
    byte-identical to what those two copies produced — this component only
    decides where the <strong> starts and ends.
*@

@if (Parts is not ({ } prefix, { } body))
{
    @Title
}
else
{
    @*  THE SEPARATING SPACE IS PART OF THE EXPRESSION, not markup between the
        two elements. Razor treats literal whitespace sitting between an element
        and an @expression as insignificant and drops it, which rendered
        "בקשה:בעיה בהתחברות…" with the colon jammed against the title.
        Emitting it from C# makes it an ordinary leading space in a text node
        after an inline element, which HTML keeps. *@
    <strong class="m-kind-title-prefix">@prefix</strong><span class="m-kind-title-rest">@($" {body}")</span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The item's kind as structured data — "בקשה", "הגשה". Rendered
    /// as the bold prefix with a colon after it. Null or blank falls through to
    /// the title's own leading label, and then to the bare title.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TypeLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The item's own name.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>How far into a title a colon may sit and still count as that
    /// title's own leading label rather than ordinary punctuation. The hosts'
    /// value, unchanged.</summary>
    private const int OwnLabelWindow = 20;

    /// <summary>The bold half and the plain half, or null when there is no
    /// label to bold and the title renders as it always did.</summary>
    /// <remarks>The second element is <c>Body</c>, not <c>Rest</c>:
    /// <c>Rest</c> is reserved by the tuple ABI (it is how ValueTuple chains
    /// past eight elements) and is rejected at any position.</remarks>
    private (string Prefix, string Body)? Parts
    {
        get
        {
            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Title)) return null;

            // A title that already opens with its own label keeps it, and that
            // label is what gets the weight — the host suppresses TypeLabel for
            // exactly these rows.
            int colon = Title.IndexOf(':');
            if (colon > 0 && colon <= OwnLabelWindow)
                return (Title[..(colon + 1)], Title[(colon + 1)..].TrimStart());

            return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(TypeLabel)
                ? null
                : ($"{TypeLabel.TrimEnd()}:", Title);
        }
    }
}
